00:02:01.240 --> 00:02:03.693
Now, with a tremendous
increase in the volume of data,

00:02:03.693 --> 00:02:05.110
from a business
standpoint, if you

00:02:05.110 --> 00:02:08.949
have to make a decision on how
to best adjust your business,

00:02:08.949 --> 00:02:11.800
both at a larger
organization level as well

00:02:11.800 --> 00:02:15.190
as a lot of smaller
tactical project

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:17.740
level and everyday
decisions level,

00:02:17.740 --> 00:02:20.320
your window of
opportunity is really low.

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:22.180
It's probably a few
hours or a few days

00:02:22.180 --> 00:02:23.980
where you have to
actually process the data

00:02:23.980 --> 00:02:29.140
and understand how best to
take actions based on the data.

00:02:29.140 --> 00:02:32.020
If you look at over
the last decade or so,

00:02:32.020 --> 00:02:34.980
how this industry
has kind of evolved,

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:37.960
it is primarily on batch data.

00:02:37.960 --> 00:02:44.920
When a large volume of data
gets collected over the day,

00:02:44.920 --> 00:02:47.800
only a small amount of data
gets used during the day.

00:02:47.800 --> 00:02:52.830
Other data is kind of uploaded
for FTP or other technologies,

00:02:52.830 --> 00:02:56.530
for a nightly job
or a weekly job.

00:02:56.530 --> 00:02:59.290
And then it is kind
of processed offline.

00:02:59.290 --> 00:03:03.370
Often, then what happens is
because of the batch nature

00:03:03.370 --> 00:03:07.570
of the analytics data,
the business decisions,

00:03:07.570 --> 00:03:11.530
what you will take, kind
of lags by a huge margin.

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:15.730
We have seen, for example, the
fraud detection risk scenarios.

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:19.140
We have seen, many times,
it takes weeks, even months,

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:22.150
for them to determine whether a
particular transaction or a set

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:25.320
of transactions were fraud or
whether a set of transactions

00:03:25.320 --> 00:03:27.430
have correlation to
determine whether it

00:03:27.430 --> 00:03:29.950
was a fraudulent
transaction or not.

00:03:29.950 --> 00:03:32.230
In today's world, more
and more organizations

00:03:32.230 --> 00:03:34.980
are trying to take
these decisions

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:36.580
in more near real time.

00:03:36.580 --> 00:03:38.380
So they are trying to
capture opportunities

00:03:38.380 --> 00:03:42.190
from things like market trend
analysis or user behavior

00:03:42.190 --> 00:03:43.110
tracking.

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:45.580
When a user for retail
is coming on your website

00:03:45.580 --> 00:03:47.500
and on your mobile
apps, you are too

00:03:47.500 --> 00:03:49.360
going to try to
predict their behavior,

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:55.000
provide more relevant
situations and recommendations

00:03:55.000 --> 00:03:57.520
or so on and so forth,
with as near real-time as

00:03:57.520 --> 00:03:59.700
possible and not
based on the user data

00:03:59.700 --> 00:04:01.470
a week back or two months back.

00:04:01.470 --> 00:04:04.680
And similarly, even in
case of reduction of risk,

00:04:04.680 --> 00:04:07.300
other than fraud detection
from bad actor identification

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:11.290
and a whole bunch of other
use cases, more and more

00:04:11.290 --> 00:04:18.910
enterprises needs to make these
decisions in near real time.

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:22.089
So with that challenge
in mind, a lot of times,

00:04:22.089 --> 00:04:27.220
the customers come to cloud and
to GCP and try to leverage some

00:04:27.220 --> 00:04:32.830
of the analytics capabilities
and AI capabilities

00:04:32.830 --> 00:04:36.220
and the surveillance ability,
which GCP is awesome at,

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:38.770
and try to see how can they
leverage those Google to get

00:04:38.770 --> 00:04:40.720
abilities to actually
solve those problems

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:42.420
in near real time.

00:04:42.420 --> 00:04:45.307
But when they look at
their on-prem system,

00:04:45.307 --> 00:04:47.640
one thing which kind of stands
in a lot of times is they

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:50.190
might be heavily
invested in Kafka.

00:04:50.190 --> 00:04:53.520
Hopefully, all of you or many
of you are familiar with Kafka.

00:04:53.520 --> 00:04:57.740
Kafka is a messaging platform.

00:04:57.740 --> 00:04:59.570
It is for pub/sub.

00:04:59.570 --> 00:05:00.780
It is a pub/sub.

00:05:00.780 --> 00:05:03.300
It's extremely scalable
messaging platform.

00:05:03.300 --> 00:05:06.150
The whole idea of messaging
and publish-subscribe

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:07.840
is nothing new.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:12.465
This has been there from the
good old days of JMS to MQ.

00:05:12.465 --> 00:05:14.760
We have seen many incarnations.

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:17.960
Coming out of LinkedIn,
I think in 2011,

00:05:17.960 --> 00:05:20.820
coming out of a few
smart software engineers

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:24.600
over at LinkedIn, what Kafka
did, it kind of reinvented

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:27.540
the whole world of
publish/subscribe

00:05:27.540 --> 00:05:32.190
and created the system which is
infinitely scalable, extremely

00:05:32.190 --> 00:05:36.500
fault-tolerant, which can
scale across multiple servers,

00:05:36.500 --> 00:05:39.210
multiple data centers,
multiple geographies.

00:05:39.210 --> 00:05:42.780
And it had this way to store
all the data infinitely

00:05:42.780 --> 00:05:44.470
in the form of logs.

00:05:44.470 --> 00:05:46.720
So it kind of created this
brand new architecture

00:05:46.720 --> 00:05:49.170
on how pub/sub
should work and which

00:05:49.170 --> 00:05:52.080
landed extremely well
as the volume of data

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:54.390
kept increasing on-prem.

00:05:54.390 --> 00:05:57.510
So a lot of enterprises
have actually

00:05:57.510 --> 00:05:59.730
standardized over
the last decade or so

00:05:59.730 --> 00:06:03.050
under Kafka, especially
using that with Apache.

00:06:03.050 --> 00:06:04.590
It has kind of
become the backbone

00:06:04.590 --> 00:06:09.570
of how all the discrete systems
are kind of coming together

00:06:09.570 --> 00:06:11.880
in the enterprise data center.

00:06:11.880 --> 00:06:14.340
So what we thought we'll
try to talk about today is,

00:06:14.340 --> 00:06:19.290
how can you leverage your
existing investment in Kafka

00:06:19.290 --> 00:06:29.110
and try to use that to do
this near real-time use cases

00:06:29.110 --> 00:06:30.600
on GCP.

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:33.160
So this is exactly
what hybrid streaming

00:06:33.160 --> 00:06:35.880
is trying to solve,
that using GCP

00:06:35.880 --> 00:06:38.710
plus Confluent and the
Confluent cloud specifically,

00:06:38.710 --> 00:06:40.710
which we are going to
talk about a little later,

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:44.130
how can you actually move this
data from your on-prem systems,

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:46.950
from your existing Kafka,
without changing anything

00:06:46.950 --> 00:06:49.890
or minimally anything,
and move the data to GCP

00:06:49.890 --> 00:06:53.070
and start leveraging the
world class serverless, NoOps,

00:06:53.070 --> 00:06:55.650
extremely scalable
and extremely powerful

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:59.120
analytics platform, which
Google offers across products

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:03.600
like BigQuery, to Dataflow,
to Looker and everything else.

