Apache Kafka, and what

00:07:13.220 --> 00:07:16.410
is a distributed event
streaming platform,

00:07:16.410 --> 00:07:18.880
and how does the whole
thing kind of fits

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:20.540
into this vision of cloud.

00:07:20.540 --> 00:07:22.968
So with that, over to you, Josh.

00:07:22.968 --> 00:07:24.260
JOSH TREICHEL: Thanks, Sarthak.

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:27.500
I think Sarthak did a
really good job describing

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:30.380
the need for real-time
data, giving some background

00:07:30.380 --> 00:07:31.580
about Kafka.

00:07:31.580 --> 00:07:32.780
And I think--

00:07:32.780 --> 00:07:36.740
I'm expecting that many of you
have at least heard or know

00:07:36.740 --> 00:07:38.120
about Kafka at a high level.

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:40.850
I'd like to just take a little
bit of time to talk about some

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:44.180
of the key features of
Kafka that we think really

00:07:44.180 --> 00:07:47.090
differentiate it and make
it a streaming platform,

00:07:47.090 --> 00:07:50.200
rather than just
pub/sub messaging.

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:55.760
Kafka is most widely known as
kind of the de facto pub/sub

00:07:55.760 --> 00:07:57.740
messaging platform.

00:07:57.740 --> 00:08:03.350
It is highly distributed and
has a ton of different use

00:08:03.350 --> 00:08:05.030
cases throughout organizations.

00:08:05.030 --> 00:08:08.180
What people don't know is
that-- or might not know

00:08:08.180 --> 00:08:11.300
is that Kafka also stores data.

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:16.100
So similar to HDF,
HDFS, Kafka stores data

00:08:16.100 --> 00:08:17.720
replicated in a durable way.

00:08:17.720 --> 00:08:19.820
And you can store as
much data as you want.

00:08:19.820 --> 00:08:21.278
So you can store
an infinite amount

00:08:21.278 --> 00:08:23.180
of data on a Kafka cluster.

00:08:23.180 --> 00:08:24.930
Kafka also allows
you to process data.

00:08:24.930 --> 00:08:28.130
So via Kafka streams,
KSQL, or even

00:08:28.130 --> 00:08:31.220
single message transformations
in the Kafka Connect framework,

00:08:31.220 --> 00:08:33.830
you can interact with
the data in real time

00:08:33.830 --> 00:08:36.309
as it's moving through.

00:08:36.309 --> 00:08:39.350
On that last slide, I mentioned
Kafka was an event streaming

00:08:39.350 --> 00:08:40.232
platform.

00:08:40.232 --> 00:08:42.690
I think a lot of people aren't
familiar with the term event

00:08:42.690 --> 00:08:44.360
streaming or
probably think like,

00:08:44.360 --> 00:08:45.620
why did you create a new term?

00:08:45.620 --> 00:08:49.250
We already have kind of existing
terms for data infrastructure.

00:08:49.250 --> 00:08:52.400
And we think that with
this new age of data

00:08:52.400 --> 00:08:54.920
and how businesses are starting
to build their business

00:08:54.920 --> 00:08:58.700
around streaming in real
time, that it really-- it

00:08:58.700 --> 00:08:59.670
does matter.

00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:02.600
And there is really
this new paradigm shift

00:09:02.600 --> 00:09:04.130
towards real-time
event streaming.

00:09:04.130 --> 00:09:07.400
And so we created
this new phrase.

00:09:07.400 --> 00:09:11.430
And this, it really has
four requirements to it.

00:09:11.430 --> 00:09:14.400
So it's built for real time.

00:09:14.400 --> 00:09:16.700
It's scalable to
all types of data.

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:19.870
It's persistent and durable,
and it's capable of enrichment.

00:09:19.870 --> 00:09:21.740
So you can interact
with the data

00:09:21.740 --> 00:09:24.010
as it's moving through
the system in real time.

00:09:24.010 --> 00:09:26.090
And there are other
existing data infrastructure

00:09:26.090 --> 00:09:28.960
tools out there that
provide a lot of these,

00:09:28.960 --> 00:09:30.710
but they don't really
provide all of them.

00:09:30.710 --> 00:09:34.160
So you could put together a
number of different systems,

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:36.740
or you could use Apache
Kafka and Confluent

00:09:36.740 --> 00:09:40.010
and get the best of breed
event streaming platform.

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:42.980
Some of the key pieces
of events streaming

00:09:42.980 --> 00:09:46.400
and specifically for this
hybrid streaming data

00:09:46.400 --> 00:09:48.380
pipelining we're
talking about is

00:09:48.380 --> 00:09:50.330
being able to connect
systems together.

00:09:50.330 --> 00:09:52.610
And in Kafka, the
way you connect

00:09:52.610 --> 00:09:57.050
systems is via, aptly named,
the Kafka Connect framework.

00:09:57.050 --> 00:09:59.210
Kafka Connect
provides a framework

00:09:59.210 --> 00:10:01.520
to easily build connectors.

00:10:01.520 --> 00:10:05.510
The ecosystem is built over
100 different connectors

00:10:05.510 --> 00:10:06.960
to different systems.

00:10:06.960 --> 00:10:09.110
So I can pull data from
pretty much anywhere,

00:10:09.110 --> 00:10:11.660
and I can push data to
pretty much anywhere.

00:10:11.660 --> 00:10:13.280
And while that
data is in Kafka, I

00:10:13.280 --> 00:10:16.970
can interact with it via
something called Kafka Streams.

00:10:16.970 --> 00:10:21.440
Kafka Streams is a Java
library to do stream processing

00:10:21.440 --> 00:10:23.150
within the Kafka ecosystem.

00:10:23.150 --> 00:10:26.690
On top of Kafka Streams,
we've built KSQL.

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:30.170
KSQL is a SQL syntax
that's an abstraction

00:10:30.170 --> 00:10:31.860
layer on top of Kafka Streams.

00:10:31.860 --> 00:10:35.750
So for non-developers,
you're able to interact

00:10:35.750 --> 00:10:40.110
with these streams
of data in real time.

00:10:40.110 --> 00:10:41.820
So with Kafka Connect
and Kafka Streams,

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:44.140
we're able to really
simplify data architecture.

00:10:44.140 --> 00:10:48.540
So instead of having tens
or hundreds of systems

00:10:48.540 --> 00:10:51.450
that need to talk to tens
or hundreds of systems,

00:10:51.450 --> 00:10:54.420
we can move data through Kafka.

00:10:54.420 --> 00:10:56.640
We can interact with that
data with Kafka Streams.

00:10:56.640 --> 00:10:57.610
We can transform it.

00:10:57.610 --> 00:10:58.973
We can merge streams.

00:10:58.973 --> 00:11:00.390
And then we can
push those streams

00:11:00.390 --> 00:11:04.710
to the systems you really want,
like BigQuery, get pulled--

00:11:04.710 --> 00:11:08.780
pull the data from to
Dataflow, push it to GCS.

00:11:08.780 --> 00:11:12.510
Now let's think about this more
from a customer's perspective.

00:11:12.510 --> 00:11:15.540
So what would this look
like in the real world?

00:11:15.540 --> 00:11:19.080
If we look on the left, we
have a very simplified view

00:11:19.080 --> 00:11:21.390
of some data infrastructure.

00:11:21.390 --> 00:11:23.940
We have a bunch of applications.

00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:25.860
They look like
microservices applications

00:11:25.860 --> 00:11:29.220
that are communicating via a
Kafka in an event-driven way.

00:11:29.220 --> 00:11:32.730
Some of those applications
are directly communicating

00:11:32.730 --> 00:11:34.240
with some data sources.

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:36.870
We also have data lakes
and other databases

00:11:36.870 --> 00:11:39.930
that are connecting to Kafka,
pushing and pulling data,

00:11:39.930 --> 00:11:41.910
maybe creating
materialized views in Kafka

00:11:41.910 --> 00:11:44.130
for those applications to read.

00:11:44.130 --> 00:11:46.770
And that's on-prem or in
some other cloud, right?

00:11:46.770 --> 00:11:49.050
And then we have where
people want to go, right?

00:11:49.050 --> 00:11:51.410
Like, the world's moving
towards the cloud.

00:11:51.410 --> 00:11:53.950
GCP has world-class analytics.

00:11:53.950 --> 00:11:56.700
So we want to, as a
customer, take part

00:11:56.700 --> 00:11:58.130
in the streaming analytics.

00:11:58.130 --> 00:12:01.110
So I'm going to let
Sarthak talk about how we

00:12:01.110 --> 00:12:04.370
can connect these two worlds.

