00:12:45.420 --> 00:12:47.400
First on the left,
you have PubSub,

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:51.990
which is Google's own version
of pub/sub messaging platform.

00:12:51.990 --> 00:12:54.480
This is serverless,
extremely fault-tolerant,

00:12:54.480 --> 00:12:56.400
extremely geo-distributed.

00:12:56.400 --> 00:12:59.790
Google manage PubSub, which is
very heavily used at Google.

00:12:59.790 --> 00:13:03.090
And a lot of customers use
it, and this is typically

00:13:03.090 --> 00:13:04.530
what their starting point is.

00:13:04.530 --> 00:13:06.930
So they would dump the
data in, and then the data

00:13:06.930 --> 00:13:11.053
can get picked up by another
subscription pub/sub topic.

00:13:11.053 --> 00:13:12.470
And then it flows
through Dataflow

00:13:12.470 --> 00:13:14.520
where there is a processing.

00:13:14.520 --> 00:13:16.350
Store the data in
BigQuery, which

00:13:16.350 --> 00:13:21.545
is Google's extremely scalable,
NoOps data warehousing tool.

00:13:21.545 --> 00:13:23.670
And then finally, they
might do some visualizations

00:13:23.670 --> 00:13:25.530
using Data Studio on BigQuery.

00:13:25.530 --> 00:13:27.300
On the other hand,
you might also

00:13:27.300 --> 00:13:30.270
want to take the same data,
process it through Dataflow,

00:13:30.270 --> 00:13:35.860
and then store it in
GCS, and store it using--

00:13:35.860 --> 00:13:38.725
in file form, as like Avro,
or Parquet, or whatnot.

00:13:38.725 --> 00:13:41.100
Keep in mind, this is a very
representative architecture.

00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:42.690
There can be a
lot of variations,

00:13:42.690 --> 00:13:46.420
from, instead of data landing
in PubSub, it can land in GCS.

00:13:46.420 --> 00:13:49.110
Or instead of just
doing visualizations,

00:13:49.110 --> 00:13:53.040
you wait on the trigger
actions using cloud functions

00:13:53.040 --> 00:13:54.690
based on the data in BigQuery.

00:13:54.690 --> 00:13:58.290
Or you might want
to use AIML, use

00:13:58.290 --> 00:14:05.150
BQML for that matter, BigQuery
ML or the ML engine in Google.

00:14:05.150 --> 00:14:07.750
Now, as Josh mentioned
that this is always

00:14:07.750 --> 00:14:10.180
kind of the desired state.

00:14:10.180 --> 00:14:12.670
This is the high
level data pipeline

00:14:12.670 --> 00:14:14.110
which you want to go towards.

00:14:14.110 --> 00:14:17.950
But in reality, it's virtualized
on the left and especially

00:14:17.950 --> 00:14:20.450
if you have invested in Kafka.

00:14:20.450 --> 00:14:25.330
So what we are trying to see is
how can you actually leverage

00:14:25.330 --> 00:14:29.530
those existing
Kafka infrastructure

00:14:29.530 --> 00:14:32.920
in pushing the data to PubSub.

00:14:32.920 --> 00:14:35.020
So the way customers
typically do

00:14:35.020 --> 00:14:39.490
it today is, PubSub has an
SDK, PubSub has REST APIs.

00:14:39.490 --> 00:14:41.920
Using those, they can
actually push the data

00:14:41.920 --> 00:14:44.680
from the different applications
and the data stores

00:14:44.680 --> 00:14:47.080
and mainframes and
whatnot to PubSub.

00:14:47.080 --> 00:14:50.510
And then PubSub, it can start
all these data pipelines.

00:14:50.510 --> 00:14:52.840
Though this works, the
challenge with this is,

00:14:52.840 --> 00:14:55.840
depending on how complex
the on-prem ecosystem is,

00:14:55.840 --> 00:14:57.530
you might need to have--

00:14:57.530 --> 00:15:00.490
include that SDK into so
many different applications

00:15:00.490 --> 00:15:03.340
and deploy it in production.

00:15:03.340 --> 00:15:05.890
And that itself can be
a daunting and a pretty

00:15:05.890 --> 00:15:08.080
complicated project,
especially if you

00:15:08.080 --> 00:15:11.170
have technologies as
old as mainframe AS/400,

00:15:11.170 --> 00:15:13.580
so on and so forth.

00:15:13.580 --> 00:15:17.320
So what we started to look at
is if an enterprise have already

00:15:17.320 --> 00:15:19.870
invested in Kafka, how
can they actually leverage

00:15:19.870 --> 00:15:23.770
that to make that generality
better wherever possible?

00:15:23.770 --> 00:15:27.520
So Confluent Cloud, which is
available as a first party

00:15:27.520 --> 00:15:30.970
service at GCP-- it is available
directly through Marketplace,

00:15:30.970 --> 00:15:34.490
with direct integration
of billings and support

00:15:34.490 --> 00:15:36.860
and integrated into
IM, networking,

00:15:36.860 --> 00:15:38.290
all that kind of stuff--

00:15:38.290 --> 00:15:40.430
they have this
awesome capability

00:15:40.430 --> 00:15:43.060
called Confluent Replicator.

00:15:43.060 --> 00:15:47.260
So if you are using
Kafka, you can then

00:15:47.260 --> 00:15:49.870
leverage that Replicator
capability feature

00:15:49.870 --> 00:15:53.410
from Confluent on top of that
and replicate the data pretty

00:15:53.410 --> 00:15:55.330
easily from on-prem to GCP.

00:15:55.330 --> 00:15:57.430
Now, all your existing
systems are already

00:15:57.430 --> 00:16:00.530
pushing data to Kafka, so you
hardly have to do anything.

00:16:00.530 --> 00:16:03.360
And then the data, once it
comes to GCP, then the same data

00:16:03.360 --> 00:16:06.460
pipeline which we talked about
earlier can be triggered on.

00:16:06.460 --> 00:16:09.390
And then the data can go into
all these different paths,

00:16:09.390 --> 00:16:11.960
from visualization to cloud
functions, to Data Studio,

00:16:11.960 --> 00:16:14.125
and so on and so forth.

00:16:14.125 --> 00:16:15.500
I'm going to hand
it over to Josh

00:16:15.500 --> 00:16:18.890
now, who is our Kafka and
Confluent expert, to walk you

00:16:18.890 --> 00:16:20.300
through what
exactly is Confluent

00:16:20.300 --> 00:16:24.140
Cloud, and Replicator, and KSQL,
and all these cool technologies

00:16:24.140 --> 00:16:25.400
that you see on the screen.

00:16:25.400 --> 00:16:27.200
Josh, over to you.

00:16:27.200 --> 00:16:28.150
JOSH TREICHEL: Cool.

00:16:28.150 --> 00:16:30.890
I think Sarthak did
a pretty good job

00:16:30.890 --> 00:16:34.220
of describing Confluent Cloud
and its tight integration

00:16:34.220 --> 00:16:35.990
to GCP.

00:16:35.990 --> 00:16:39.610
The idea here is really that
for existing Kafka users,

00:16:39.610 --> 00:16:42.650
we're trying to simplify
and ease their adoption

00:16:42.650 --> 00:16:44.120
journey to the cloud.

00:16:44.120 --> 00:16:45.770
And so as Sarthak
mentioned, there's

00:16:45.770 --> 00:16:47.550
a bunch of different
ways you can do this.

00:16:47.550 --> 00:16:48.710
There's a number of--

00:16:48.710 --> 00:16:51.080
you could go point to
point from a database

00:16:51.080 --> 00:16:52.430
directly to BigQuery.

00:16:52.430 --> 00:16:54.020
But we think that
for Kafka users,

00:16:54.020 --> 00:16:57.080
since Kafka is oftentimes
kind of the way

00:16:57.080 --> 00:17:00.440
that those systems connect,
that tapping into that existing

00:17:00.440 --> 00:17:03.350
Kafka infrastructure,
getting that-- creating

00:17:03.350 --> 00:17:07.520
that persistent bridge, that
real-time bridge to the cloud,

00:17:07.520 --> 00:17:10.400
and then being able to integrate
directly to GCP first party

00:17:10.400 --> 00:17:13.880
services, and start building
applications with the data

00:17:13.880 --> 00:17:17.530
from on-prem in the cloud
is really compelling.

00:17:17.530 --> 00:17:19.560
And so Confluent Cloud,
as Sarthak mentioned,

00:17:19.560 --> 00:17:21.740
is a fully managed service.

00:17:21.740 --> 00:17:24.680
And so this makes the
journey a bit easier, right?

00:17:24.680 --> 00:17:28.099
So you're not having
to manage Kafka on-prem

00:17:28.099 --> 00:17:29.570
and then manage
Kafka in the cloud.

00:17:29.570 --> 00:17:33.190
We take that burden
away from you.

00:17:33.190 --> 00:17:35.440
I wanted to talk a little
bit about the two components

00:17:35.440 --> 00:17:38.070
on that last slide, Confluent
Cloud and Replicator.

00:17:38.070 --> 00:17:40.428
So Confluent Replicator,
on that slide,

00:17:40.428 --> 00:17:42.220
it looked like it was
actually kind of part

00:17:42.220 --> 00:17:44.650
of both sides of that, right?

00:17:44.650 --> 00:17:48.190
So it was part on-prem
and part in the cloud.

00:17:48.190 --> 00:17:50.800
It's actually a part of
the Connect framework

00:17:50.800 --> 00:17:53.720
or uses the Connect
framework and is a connector.

00:17:53.720 --> 00:17:56.260
It's a complicated connector
that has a bunch of cool stuff

00:17:56.260 --> 00:17:58.450
that makes it super resilient.

00:17:58.450 --> 00:18:02.260
But it's a connector that either
runs on the origin cluster

00:18:02.260 --> 00:18:03.500
or at the destination.

00:18:03.500 --> 00:18:05.650
So it's either pulling
data across the network

00:18:05.650 --> 00:18:07.420
or it's pushing data
across the network.

00:18:07.420 --> 00:18:09.940
Typically, we have
it pull data, but you

00:18:09.940 --> 00:18:11.810
can run it in either pattern.

00:18:11.810 --> 00:18:14.860
And so this Confluent
Replicator allows

00:18:14.860 --> 00:18:19.100
us a really nice clean story
to tap into existing Kafka

00:18:19.100 --> 00:18:21.550
infrastructure without really
having to touch anything.

00:18:21.550 --> 00:18:23.710
So we don't have to go
touch those databases.

00:18:23.710 --> 00:18:27.310
We're only hooking into the
existing Kafka installation

00:18:27.310 --> 00:18:28.382
on-prem.

00:18:28.382 --> 00:18:30.810
And Confluent Cloud,
as Sarthak mentioned,

00:18:30.810 --> 00:18:32.800
it's a fully managed service.

00:18:32.800 --> 00:18:36.120
If you've ever
managed Kafka before,

00:18:36.120 --> 00:18:37.590
you know it can be challenging.

00:18:37.590 --> 00:18:40.150
So it's a distributed
system of Kafka brokers.

00:18:40.150 --> 00:18:42.270
It's a distributed
system of ZooKeepers.

00:18:42.270 --> 00:18:44.603
Kafka Connect is a
distributed system.

00:18:44.603 --> 00:18:46.020
And so there's a
lot of complexity

00:18:46.020 --> 00:18:48.075
here that we hide from you.

00:18:48.075 --> 00:18:49.950
So you don't know how
many brokers there are.

00:18:49.950 --> 00:18:51.810
You don't know about ZooKeeper.

00:18:51.810 --> 00:18:54.780
When you're running the Connect
framework, this is from a UI

00:18:54.780 --> 00:18:56.490
where you just point and click.

00:18:56.490 --> 00:18:58.920
So we really-- we think
that this is a whole new way

00:18:58.920 --> 00:19:01.510
to run Kafka, kind of
a cloud native Kafka,

00:19:01.510 --> 00:19:03.310
that's super compelling.

00:19:03.310 --> 00:19:05.230
And it's not just Kafka
that we're running.

00:19:05.230 --> 00:19:06.570
We're running Schema Registry.

00:19:06.570 --> 00:19:07.838
We're running KSQL.

00:19:07.838 --> 00:19:09.880
As I mentioned, we're
running connectors for you.

00:19:09.880 --> 00:19:11.970
So if you want to
connect to BigQuery,

00:19:11.970 --> 00:19:14.970
you don't have to figure out how
much data you're moving there.

00:19:14.970 --> 00:19:17.070
You just go to the
UI, say you want

00:19:17.070 --> 00:19:20.190
to connect this topic to
BigQuery, and it starts moving.

00:19:20.190 --> 00:19:22.080
And then we allow you
to store as much data

00:19:22.080 --> 00:19:24.570
you want on that Kafka
cluster as you need.

00:19:24.570 --> 00:19:27.790
So you can store an infinite
amount of data in those topics.

00:19:27.790 --> 00:19:31.800
So adding these two features,
if we take a step down

00:19:31.800 --> 00:19:34.130
from the more
architecture slide,

00:19:34.130 --> 00:19:36.660
if we think about
existing customers

00:19:36.660 --> 00:19:40.080
that have on-premise Kafka
infrastructure, whether it's

00:19:40.080 --> 00:19:44.190
open source Apache Kafka,
Confluent Enterprise,

00:19:44.190 --> 00:19:46.320
they can use
Confluent Replicator.

00:19:46.320 --> 00:19:47.730
They can run it on-prem.

00:19:47.730 --> 00:19:49.260
They can run it in GCP.

00:19:49.260 --> 00:19:52.530
And now they can create this
real-time stream of data

00:19:52.530 --> 00:19:55.050
to Confluent Cloud, once the
data is in Confluent Cloud,

00:19:55.050 --> 00:19:57.330
take advantage of those
fully managed connectors

00:19:57.330 --> 00:19:59.657
and just start pumping
data into the services

00:19:59.657 --> 00:20:00.990
that they're most interested in.

00:20:00.990 --> 00:20:05.610
They can also have applications
that start directly integrating

00:20:05.610 --> 00:20:07.350
with Confluent Cloud.

00:20:07.350 --> 00:20:10.290
So building microservices
in an event-driven way

00:20:10.290 --> 00:20:12.830
and using Kafka as the
mechanism to do that

00:20:12.830 --> 00:20:14.620
is a super common pattern.

00:20:14.620 --> 00:20:16.770
So now you can have
Confluent Cloud there

00:20:16.770 --> 00:20:19.810
as your event-driven
microservices Kafka.

00:20:19.810 --> 00:20:22.590
Let's talk about a
couple use cases here.

00:20:22.590 --> 00:20:25.860
To start, just wanted
to talk about the fact

00:20:25.860 --> 00:20:30.440
that this is a super kind of
simple, horizontal use case

00:20:30.440 --> 00:20:32.810
that can be used
practically for anything.

00:20:32.810 --> 00:20:36.360
As Sarthak mentioned
earlier, any use case where

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:40.320
you need real-time data
that doesn't live on GCP,

00:20:40.320 --> 00:20:42.090
this is pretty well suited for.

00:20:42.090 --> 00:20:45.960
We're taking advantage of the
fact that something like--

00:20:45.960 --> 00:20:49.470
it's something like 70%,
75% of Fortune 500 companies

00:20:49.470 --> 00:20:51.490
already run Kafka.

00:20:51.490 --> 00:20:53.010
So the assumption
here is that that

00:20:53.010 --> 00:20:55.740
can be a vast source of
data that we can easily

00:20:55.740 --> 00:20:59.010
tap into and start creating
this real-time data pipeline.

00:20:59.010 --> 00:21:01.990
Once we have this flow
of real-time data,

00:21:01.990 --> 00:21:04.120
we can start building
solutions with it, right?

00:21:04.120 --> 00:21:05.880
So if the flow is
from a mainframe,

00:21:05.880 --> 00:21:07.560
and you have financial
data, now you

00:21:07.560 --> 00:21:10.290
can start building some
fraud analytics on that.

00:21:10.290 --> 00:21:13.810
If you're a telecom, you
probably want to think about,

00:21:13.810 --> 00:21:16.970
do I have anomalous
activity at my antennas?

00:21:16.970 --> 00:21:18.720
That might be something
you're looking at.

00:21:18.720 --> 00:21:21.240
But effectively, the
pattern we're talking about

00:21:21.240 --> 00:21:23.730
is you're building the
data infrastructure

00:21:23.730 --> 00:21:25.350
to get data to GCP.

00:21:25.350 --> 00:21:29.160
And then you, the customer,
can build the business solution

00:21:29.160 --> 00:21:30.580
that you want to build.

00:21:30.580 --> 00:21:33.435
One really cool pattern
that we see a lot is

00:21:33.435 --> 00:21:34.560
using this for multi cloud.

00:21:37.500 --> 00:21:39.600
A lot of customers
have already--

00:21:39.600 --> 00:21:42.480
they live in one
cloud, but they started

00:21:42.480 --> 00:21:45.420
to think like, we need to find
the best of breed technologies

00:21:45.420 --> 00:21:47.310
across the clouds and use that.

00:21:47.310 --> 00:21:50.190
And in the data analytics
world, that's GCP.

00:21:50.190 --> 00:21:53.940
So a lot of customers want
to move to GCP for BigQuery,

00:21:53.940 --> 00:21:57.100
but the data they want to use in
BigQuery might not live there.

00:21:57.100 --> 00:21:58.170
So if the data--

00:21:58.170 --> 00:22:00.730
if that's a Kafka user, we can
use that exact same pattern,

00:22:00.730 --> 00:22:01.230
right?

00:22:01.230 --> 00:22:04.080
Hook up Replicator,
Confluent Cloud, and GCP,

00:22:04.080 --> 00:22:05.530
and just start feeding BigQuery.

00:22:05.530 --> 00:22:08.543
So your system of record may
live in a different cloud,

00:22:08.543 --> 00:22:09.960
but you can actually
use that data

00:22:09.960 --> 00:22:13.560
in pretty much near real
time in GCP, in BigQuery.

00:22:13.560 --> 00:22:16.050
We actually have customers
that are kind of doing this.

00:22:16.050 --> 00:22:19.160
So Unity Technologies,
who GCP and Confluent

00:22:19.160 --> 00:22:22.440
have done a joint webinar
with, moved from another cloud

00:22:22.440 --> 00:22:25.013
to GCP by building this bridge.

00:22:25.013 --> 00:22:26.430
And they moved to
GCP because they

00:22:26.430 --> 00:22:28.735
wanted Dataflow and BigQuery.

00:22:28.735 --> 00:22:30.360
And they were a Kafka
customer, so this

00:22:30.360 --> 00:22:33.290
is the pattern they used.

00:22:33.290 --> 00:22:35.750
So Sarthak, maybe
you can just walk us

00:22:35.750 --> 00:22:39.780
through how customers might
use this for AI and ML.

00:22:39.780 --> 00:22:41.780
SARTHAK GANGOPADHYAY:
Yeah, no, thank you, Josh.

00:22:41.780 --> 00:22:44.990
So this particular architecture
diagram which you are seeing,

00:22:44.990 --> 00:22:48.840
this is actually a
customer implementation

00:22:48.840 --> 00:22:50.960
for a customer in
the banking space

00:22:50.960 --> 00:22:54.800
who wanted to handle a
better fraud detection, more

00:22:54.800 --> 00:22:58.880
near real time fraud detection,
using GCP's capabilities.

00:22:58.880 --> 00:23:01.610
But they had tremendous
data gravity on-prem

00:23:01.610 --> 00:23:05.590
because they had a lot of
data in mainframes, in Hadoop,

00:23:05.590 --> 00:23:08.900
in Oracle, Teradata, et
cetera, lying on-prem.

00:23:08.900 --> 00:23:12.890
Plus, they had a whole bunch
of data from different stores,

00:23:12.890 --> 00:23:16.310
to mobile devices, to web,
which is constantly coming in.

00:23:16.310 --> 00:23:20.212
And all the systems are set
up to receive that data.

00:23:20.212 --> 00:23:21.670
Thankfully, they
were already using

00:23:21.670 --> 00:23:24.930
Kafka to communicate across
all these disparate systems

00:23:24.930 --> 00:23:27.550
as a message broker on-prem.

00:23:27.550 --> 00:23:30.133
And what we did was we
deployed Confluent Cloud

00:23:30.133 --> 00:23:31.800
and used Confluent
Replicator, like what

00:23:31.800 --> 00:23:35.550
we have been talking about, to
move some of the data to GCP.

00:23:35.550 --> 00:23:38.190
Now, as a first step,
what they're doing

00:23:38.190 --> 00:23:40.110
is they're actually
taking the full data which

00:23:40.110 --> 00:23:42.090
is something every day.

00:23:42.090 --> 00:23:44.730
Rather, in near real-time,
as more data is coming in,

00:23:44.730 --> 00:23:46.800
they're actually flowing
it through Dataflow

00:23:46.800 --> 00:23:50.740
for doing some processing,
storing the full data source

00:23:50.740 --> 00:23:52.410
in a stream in BigQuery.

00:23:52.410 --> 00:23:55.980
Because in BigQuery now, you
can do a lot faster analysis

00:23:55.980 --> 00:23:57.990
across those terabytes
or petabytes of data

00:23:57.990 --> 00:24:00.870
than what you could have
on-prem, especially across all

00:24:00.870 --> 00:24:02.400
those discrete systems.

00:24:02.400 --> 00:24:04.050
And then they're
doing visualizations

00:24:04.050 --> 00:24:05.950
of the data using Looker.

00:24:05.950 --> 00:24:09.125
But probably, more interesting
is they are actually doing--

00:24:09.125 --> 00:24:12.370
in the Confluent Cloud,
they're actually sorting out

00:24:12.370 --> 00:24:14.340
some specific data streams.

00:24:14.340 --> 00:24:18.540
And those data streams, they
are training some fraud models,

00:24:18.540 --> 00:24:21.610
and they're actually
passing it to those fraud--

00:24:21.610 --> 00:24:23.500
they're deploying
those fraud models.

00:24:23.500 --> 00:24:25.380
And then they're
sending those data

00:24:25.380 --> 00:24:27.410
to run those fraud
models to determine

00:24:27.410 --> 00:24:31.280
what of those transactions
have any fraudulent behavior

00:24:31.280 --> 00:24:32.450
in them.

00:24:32.450 --> 00:24:34.420
And then finally,
they're using--

00:24:34.420 --> 00:24:37.590
actually sending the
data to a Kafka topic

00:24:37.590 --> 00:24:40.840
on running on Confluent Cloud,
so that they can then send back

00:24:40.840 --> 00:24:46.060
their feedback downstream
to their on-prem system,

